I’ll inspect the pattern, metadata, and the frozen PXRD workflow/tooling first, then build a phase inventory and refine only models that remain physically supported. I’ll keep the final exports tied to the saved GSAS-II project and verify their consistency.

The chemistry points first to ZnWO₄, but the raw scan must decide whether residual ZnO/WO₃ or an off-stoichiometric zinc tungstate is present. I’m now extracting peak families and querying the frozen COD index for all Zn–W–O candidates plus the two precursors, before any refinement.

The peak positions coherently match monoclinic wolframite ZnWO₄ after a small cell contraction/angle correction. Several weaker families near 26.7°, 28.9°, and 33–34° could either be ZnWO₄ reflections or residual WO₃, so I’m keeping WO₃ and ZnO on the watch list and will test their scales under the same profile/background model. The triclinic COD “ZnWO₄” records are Cu–Zn solid-solution sources and are not physically appropriate primary models here.

Both physically plausible WO₃ records converge to about 17.3–17.5 wt% WO₃ and reduce Rwp from 17.6% to about 11.1%; the coherent WO₃ triplet and higher-angle families make that phase unambiguous. I’m now comparing the monoclinic/triclinic WO₃ records under identical nuisance terms and re-testing ZnO only after the WO₃ contribution is present, so overlap cannot inflate a false ZnO scale.

The three-phase inventory is now stable at roughly 74–75% ZnWO₄, 18% WO₃, and 7–8% ZnO. Profile trials show a common broad baseline plus a reproducible WO₃ strain term and a ZnO size term; ZnWO₄-specific size/strain collapses to limits and is being pruned. I’m now challenging preferred orientation and atomic parameters against that pruned baseline, with physical bounds and replay checks.

The source-screen exposed an important normalization issue: the raw COD records carry incompatible displacement conventions (ZnO anisotropic ADPs, WO₃ zero ADPs, ZnWO₄ mixed Uiso), while the supplied generator registers all models as neutral, isotropic Uiso = 0.005 Å² before applying specimen-specific Uiso. I’m rebuilding the same published structures with that documented normalization and will reconverge the inventory; this should separate genuine intensity effects from source-ADP artifacts.

The standardized source pool is the key checkpoint now: it removes source-file ADP/occupancy inconsistencies before any further structural refinement. I’m verifying that pool, then I’ll rebuild both plausible monoclinic WO₃ variants under the same nuisance model and carry only the physically stronger one into the final project.

Both normalized WO₃ parents converge to the same monoclinic metric, and ZnO remains at about 7.2 wt% in either fit. The neutron-derived WO₃ parent is slightly better so far, but the difference is small; I’m now equalizing background, roughness, and profile treatment before judging that source choice.

The common displacement parameter is genuinely supported for the major ZnWO₄ phase (refining to ~0.0031 Å²); the minor-phase displacement parameters are effectively unchanged and do not improve the fit, so I’m pruning those. I’m now testing coordinates in chemically controlled blocks—heavy sites first, oxygen sites second—so a nominal residual gain cannot quietly create implausible polyhedra.

The nuisance-model challenges are now separating cleanly: ZnWO₄ shows a small but coherent [001] March–Dollase correction, while WO₃ and ZnO texture terms are source-dependent or effectively neutral and will be excluded. The retained coordinate shifts are also within the generator’s physical perturbation scale; oxygen coordinates in minor phases were rejected because they moved too far without a stable fit gain.

The physically constrained baseline is now stable on replay. The major phase needs no additional size/strain term (the strain trial runs negative, so it is rejected); WO₃ supports a replay-stable [110] uniaxial microstrain, and ZnO supports isotropic size broadening. I’m at the final audit stage now: clearing dormant constraints, checking residuals for a missed crystalline phase, validating bond geometry/background/profile positivity, then exporting directly from the audited project.

The project now replays independently with zero profile-review flags, and its regenerated phase fractions match the submitted values exactly. The geometry audit also passes (octahedral W/Zn environments and tetrahedral ZnO are all chemically sensible). I’m doing one last submission-level consistency check—CIF rounding/ADPs, normalized fractions, and full-grid `fit.csv`—before handing it over.

Completed and independently replay-validated.

Crystalline fractions:

- ZnWO₄ wolframite: 74.01 wt%
- Monoclinic WO₃: 19.18 wt%
- Wurtzite ZnO: 6.82 wt%

Fit: Rwp 5.258%, Rp 4.044%. Fractions are normalized over modeled crystalline material; amorphous content was not quantified.

